**Read:** The full solution chapters assigned by [Explain solution formation through interactions and thermodynamic conditions](../../../chemistry/general-chemistry/solutions/Solvation-mixing-and-solubility.lingua#r_4PY91PEG2715V7F8H7MVGP4S95); [Cleaning and surface properties — Mauro Taborelli](../sources/Cleaning-and-Surface-Properties-Taborelli.lingua#r_1RDARBEFXDAW9Y2DNTJ2QBEP2E), solvent cleaning.

For an ideal unchanged solid with equilibrium mass concentration csat in a liquid volume V, initially at c0<csat, the available dissolution capacity is V(csat−c0). The maximum removable mass by this mechanism is the smaller of that capacity and the available residue mass. This account excludes reaction, adsorption, solvent loss and changing phases.

If 12 mg of residue meets 10 mL of liquid with csat=1 mg/mL and c0=0.2 mg/mL, at most 8 mg can dissolve in that bath under the stated conditions; 4 mg remains even after unlimited equilibration time. Better mixing alone does not increase this equilibrium capacity.

**Work:** Close the solid/liquid mass account and compare increasing liquid volume, replacing a bath and changing the equilibrium chemistry. State how evidence of a new solid phase or complex would change the model. Separate a measured slow approach to equilibrium from a genuine low-solubility limit.

